diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's premise is incorrect — there is no fault in the network.
A thorough live inspection of the ospf_enterprise_static topology found all
components fully operational at the time of investigation.

router_core_3 is running and healthy: FRR is active, its OSPF process has
router-ID 172.16.0.3 and is advertising its three directly-connected
point-to-point subnets (172.16.0.2/31, 172.16.0.4/31, 172.16.0.10/31)
correctly into area 0. It has received and installed OSPF-learned routes to
all other prefixes in the topology.

switch_server_access (the L3 aggregation device for the server farm) has
10.200.0.0/24 directly connected on br0 and is advertising it via OSPF.
router_core_3 receives this advertisement and propagates it toward
router_core_1 and router_core_2, which both show a valid OSPF route to
10.200.0.0/24. The end-to-end forwarding path from host_1_1_1_1 through
switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 → router_core_3 → switch_server_access to
every server in the farm is intact.

Live pings from host_1_1_1_1 to web_server_0 (10.200.0.3), web_server_1
(10.200.0.4), web_server_2 (10.200.0.5), web_server_3 (10.200.0.6), and
dns_server (10.200.0.2) all completed with 0% packet loss, as did pings from
host_2_1_1_1 and from the server farm's own devices to each other.

Root cause: The reported outage does not exist in the current lab state; the
ticket is based on a false premise.

Corrective action: No network change is required. Advise the user that the
servers are reachable and router_core_3 is fully operational. If the user
experienced a transient outage immediately after maintenance, it was likely
self-healing OSPF reconvergence (which completes in seconds); no remediation
is needed.
=== END ===
